For a symmetric physical stress tensor, f = div(tau) has components
\begin{aligned}
f_r&=\partial_r\tau_{rr}+\frac1r\partial_\phi\tau_{r\phi}
+\partial_z\tau_{rz}+\frac{\tau_{rr}-\tau_{\phi\phi}}r,\\
f_\phi&=\partial_r\tau_{r\phi}+\frac1r\partial_\phi\tau_{\phi\phi}
+\partial_z\tau_{\phi z}+\frac{2\tau_{r\phi}}r,\\
f_z&=\partial_r\tau_{rz}+\frac1r\partial_\phi\tau_{\phi z}
+\partial_z\tau_{zz}+\frac{\tau_{rz}}r.
\end{aligned}
The acceleration contribution is f/rho. Pressure contributes −grad(p) separately. These divergence formulas allow spatially variable mu and zeta when stress derivatives include their variation. In the Python routine, partials[...,i,j,k] is the coordinate derivative k of physical stress component (i,j).

\begin{aligned}H_{x,i,j+1/2}^{n+1/2}&=H_{x,i,j+1/2}^{n-1/2}-\frac{\Delta t}{\Delta y}(E_{i,j+1}^{n}-E_{i,j}^{n}),\\H_{y,i+1/2,j}^{n+1/2}&=H_{y,i+1/2,j}^{n-1/2}+\frac{\Delta t}{\Delta x}(E_{i+1,j}^{n}-E_{i,j}^{n}),\\E_{i,j}^{n+1}&=E_{i,j}^{n}+\Delta t\left(\frac{H_{y,i+1/2,j}^{n+1/2}-H_{y,i-1/2,j}^{n+1/2}}{\Delta x}-\frac{H_{x,i,j+1/2}^{n+1/2}-H_{x,i,j-1/2}^{n+1/2}}{\Delta y}\right).\end{aligned}
Ez lives at nodes and integer times; Hx is displaced by half a y cell and Hy by half an x cell, both at half-integer times. Each step advances H first and then E. Initial E at time zero and H at −Δt/2 are sampled from analytic fields; assigning both fields time zero would introduce a first-order initialization error.
Periodic arrays omit duplicate endpoints and wrap differences. PEC walls fix tangential Ez=0 and update only interior electric nodes. Half weights for boundary magnetic samples make the weighted inner products compatible with discrete summation by parts and the energy identity.

\begin{aligned}U_*^n&=\frac12\lVert E^n\rVert_h^2+\frac12\langle H^{n-1/2},H^{n+1/2}\rangle_h=\mathrm{constant},\\\bar H^n&=(H^{n-1/2}+H^{n+1/2})/2,\\ U_{\rm sync}^n&=\tfrac12(\lVert E^n\rVert_h^2+\lVert\bar H^n\rVert_h^2),\\ U_*^n&=U_{\rm sync}^n-\frac{\Delta t^2}{8}\lVert\operatorname{curl}_h E^n\rVert_h^2.\end{aligned}
Inner products include cell area and the appropriate PEC boundary weights. U*, containing magnetic fields from two different half times, is conserved up to roundoff in the closed periodic/PEC systems. The strict CFL inequality also ensures positivity of this modified energy. Usync, using magnetic fields averaged to an integer time, is a second-order approximation to physical energy but is not exactly invariant.
For unit-amplitude continuum fields the total energies are LxLy/2 for the traveling periodic wave and LxLy/8 for the cavity standing wave. Twelve periods are used to track relative U* drift and the variation of Usync; the error against the analytic physical energy must decrease at approximately second order under grid/time refinement. Tests also check U* conservation with compatible random fields, beyond single eigenmodes.

\begin{aligned}E_z&=E_x+E_y,\\\partial_t E_x+\gamma_{x,E}E_x&=\partial_xH_y,&\partial_t E_y+\gamma_{y,E}E_y&=-\partial_yH_x,\\\partial_t H_x+\gamma_{y,H}H_x&=-\partial_yE_z,&\partial_t H_y+\gamma_{x,H}H_y&=\partial_xE_z,\quad\epsilon=\mu=1.\end{aligned}
In the implemented vacuum 2D TMz square, γx layers occupy the left/right sides and γy layers the bottom/top sides. All outer caps are PEC. Both directional rates are nonzero in the four overlap corners. Eₓ and Eᵧ are auxiliary splits of Ez, not physical x/y electric components. Each rate is sampled at its own electric or staggered magnetic location; damping uses the trapezoidal time update.
One packet family targets ordinary sides; another targets corners obliquely. Checks establish more than a decrease in global energy: all four outgoing fluxes and contributions in all four corners are recorded. Physical layer thickness, strength and shape stay fixed during refinement, so changing resolution does not change the physical layer.

\begin{aligned}W_n={}&\Delta t\langle\bar E_n,\gamma_{x,E}\bar E_{x,n}+\gamma_{y,E}\bar E_{y,n}\rangle_h\\&+\frac{\Delta t}{2}\langle H_x^{n+1/2},\gamma_{y,H}(\bar H_{x,n}+\bar H_{x,n+1})\rangle_h\\&+\frac{\Delta t}{2}\langle H_y^{n+1/2},\gamma_{x,H}(\bar H_{y,n}+\bar H_{y,n+1})\rangle_h,\\U_*^{n+1}-U_*^n+W_n={}&0.\end{aligned}
The averaged fields and modified energy U* retain their definitions above, but work now includes all four electric-x/y and magnetic-x/y terms. Magnetic work uses three consecutive half times. Every term is tallied directly from fields and rates, not reconstructed from energy loss. Auxiliary work can be locally signed and is distinct from positive physical heating. This does not claim a new general stability theorem for arbitrary split fields.
\begin{gathered}S_x=-E_zH_y,\quad S_y=E_zH_x,\\\Phi(t)=\int_{y_B}^{y_T}(S_x(x_R,y,t)-S_x(x_L,y,t))\,dy+\int_{x_L}^{x_R}(S_y(x,y_T,t)-S_y(x,y_B,t))\,dx,\\U_{\rm physical}(t)-U_{\rm physical}(0)+\int_0^t\Phi(\tau)\,d\tau=0.\end{gathered}
The physical rectangle inside the PML sums right-minus-left and top-minus-bottom Poynting fluxes and compares them with interior energy change. Electric corner nodes use quarter quadrature weights and edge nodes half weights, avoiding double counting. The exact full-domain discrete work identity is checked separately from the interpolated/time-integrated physical-region balance.

\begin{gathered}\omega^2=(k^2+k_y^2)/\epsilon_1,\quad q=\sqrt{\epsilon_2\omega^2-k_y^2},\quad\Re q\ge0,\ \Im q\ge0,\\Y_1=k/\epsilon_1,\quad Y_2=q/\epsilon_2,\quad r_H=\frac{Y_1-Y_2}{Y_1+Y_2},\quad t_H=1+r_H,\\R=|r_H|^2,\qquad T=\frac{\Re Y_2}{Y_1}|t_H|^2,\quad E_x=-\frac{k_y}{\epsilon\omega}H_z,\quad E_y=\frac{k_x}{\epsilon\omega}H_z.\end{gathered}\tan\theta_B=\sqrt{\epsilon_2/\epsilon_1},\quad r_H(\theta_B)=0;\qquad\theta_c=\sin^{-1}\sqrt{\epsilon_2/\epsilon_1}\quad(\epsilon_1>\epsilon_2).
The convention is exp[i(kx x+ky y−ωt)]. rH,tH are Hz amplitude coefficients; reflected E uses signed kx=−k and transmitted E uses kx=q. The tangential Ey reflection sign is distinguished from the polarization-basis amplitude convention. TIR takes q=iκ with κ>0, so fields decay to the right.
\begin{gathered}u=(k-k_0)/b,\\A_H(k)=\begin{cases}C\exp\!\left[-\frac{w^2(k-k_0)^2}{2}-\frac{u^8}{1-u^2}\right],&|u|<1,\\0,&|u|\ge1,\end{cases}\\U_{\rm inc}=\pi L_y\int|A_H(k)|^2\,dk\approx\frac{L_yP}{2}\sum_m a_m^2,\\a_m=A_H(k_m)\Delta k,\qquad\Delta k=2\pi/P,\\R_\infty=\frac{\int |A_H|^2R(k)\,dk}{\int |A_H|^2\,dk},\qquad T_\infty=\frac{\int |A_H|^2T(k)\,dk}{\int |A_H|^2\,dk}.\end{gathered}

\begin{gathered}\Phi_X(t)=\frac{L_y}{2}\Re\!\left[E_{y,X}^{\rm centred}(t)H_X(t)^*\right],\\R_{\cal W}=-\frac{\int_{\cal W}\Phi_{\rm ref}\,dt}{\int_{\cal W}\Phi_{\rm inc}\,dt},\qquad T_{\cal W}=\frac{\int_{\cal W}\Phi_{\rm tr}\,dt}{\int_{\cal W}\Phi_{\rm inc}\,dt}.\end{gathered}
Interface and homogeneous-medium runs use the same Yee update. Subtracting their H/E at the left monitor isolates the reflected component, avoiding ordinary incident dispersion obscuring a weak Brewster return. Incident/reflected left flux and transmitted right flux are integrated in time. H is spatially averaged to each face and E centered to integer time. Finite-window ratios are recorded separately from their gaps to asymptotic spectral R∞,T∞.
\begin{gathered}\widehat H(\omega_0)=\int_{\cal W}H(t)e^{i\omega_0t}\,dt,\\r_{H,h}^{\rm DFT}=\frac{\widehat H_{\rm ref}}{\widehat H_{\rm inc}}e^{-2ik_hd},\\\kappa_h=-\frac{\log|\widehat H(x_2)/\widehat H(x_1)|}{x_2-x_1}.\end{gathered}\begin{gathered}\Omega_0=\frac{2}{\Delta t}\sin(\omega_0\Delta t/2),\\k_h=\frac{2}{\Delta x}\sin^{-1}\!\left[\frac{\Delta x}{2}\sqrt{\epsilon_1\Omega_0^2-K_y^2}\right].\end{gathered}

The complete TIR support lies beyond the critical angle: every mode has R=1 and zero normal time-average T. Evanescent fields and transient stored energy still exist on the right. Signed incoming/outgoing transient flux, maximum right-side storage and final storage are recorded separately; right-side stored energy is not called transmittance.
\begin{gathered}U_*^n=\tfrac12\bigl(\|H^n\|_h^2+\langle E^{n-1/2},\epsilon E^{n+1/2}\rangle_h\bigr),\quad U_*^n=U_*^0,\\\bar E^n=(E^{n-1/2}+E^{n+1/2})/2,\quad U_{\rm phys}^n-U_*^n=\tfrac18\|E^{n+1/2}-E^{n-1/2}\|_{\epsilon,h}^2,\\\Delta U_{[x_L,x_R]}+\int(\Phi_{x_R}-\Phi_{x_L})\,dt=0.\end{gathered}
Modified discrete energy is distinct from physical energy using centered E. Interior flux is evaluated directly from fields, not reconstructed from energy loss. Splitting interface Ey energy assigns ε₁/2 and ε₂/2 to the two sides, retaining the global averaged coefficient. Field accuracy is assessed at a fixed interaction time; energy and flux continue to a later end time. End-time field error is reported separately.

Only separations |s| < Lz are required. Extend the kernel periodically over a separation interval of length 2Lz and set k_z,ell = pi ell/Lz. For k > 0 the continuous integral is
\widetilde{\mathcal G}_{k,\ell}
=\int_{-L_z}^{L_z}\mathcal G_k(s)e^{-ik_{z,\ell}s}\,ds
=-\frac{4\pi G}{k^2+k_{z,\ell}^2}
\left[1-(-1)^\ell e^{-kL_z}\right].
It follows by integrating the even exponential against a cosine. The square-bracket factor distinguishes the slab kernel from the ordinary fully periodic Poisson multiplier. Equivalently, doubling z and adding a negative image density at displacement Lz with horizontal-mode factor exp(−k Lz) produces this factor before applying the ordinary nonzero Poisson multiplier.
For the k = 0 horizontal mode, the continuous coefficient is
\widetilde{\mathcal G}_{0,\ell}=
\begin{cases}
2\pi G L_z^2,&\ell=0,\\
\dfrac{4\pi G[(-1)^\ell-1]}{k_{z,\ell}^2},&\ell\ne0.
\end{cases}
The all-zero coefficient fixes an additive potential constant in this convention. Other constant gauges give the same force. Dropping the entire horizontal k = 0 mode, however, removes the physical mean sheet and is incorrect. Our slab solver does not subtract the mean density.

Let dz = Lz/Nz and s_j be the 2Nz signed vertical lags. The multiplier for the discrete quadrature is
\widetilde{\mathcal G}^{\rm sampled}_{k,\ell}
=\Delta z\sum_{j=0}^{2N_z-1}\mathcal G_k(s_j)
 e^{-2\pi i j\ell/(2N_z)}.
This is exactly dz times the DFT of the sampled kernel. It is not, at finite Nz, identical to the continuous integral above. For k > 0 its real value can also be evaluated by the geometric series
\widetilde{\mathcal G}^{\rm sampled}_{k,\ell}
=-\frac{2\pi G\Delta z}{k}
\frac{\sinh(k\Delta z)}{\cosh(k\Delta z)-\cos(\pi\ell/N_z)}
\left[1-(-1)^\ell e^{-kL_z}\right].
Use a separate k = 0 branch to avoid cancellation and division by zero. Expanding sinh, cosh, and cos recovers the continuous coefficient for fixed resolved modes as dz tends to zero. Tests measure this convergence. The distinction prevents labeling a continuum coefficient as the exact finite sum of sampled values.
slab_green_transform exposes both sampled and continuum representations. slab_potential defaults to the sampled kernel, so its FFT result matches an independent direct vertical quadrature. The continuum option applies the analytic coefficient instead. This is a small independently written kernel laboratory, not a reproduction of all numerical choices or the interstellar-medium simulations in the paper.

Evaluate this two-point flux between neighboring grid nodes, then take the right-minus-left flux difference to update the conserved state. Written in the two-dimensional notation of this implementation, Kuya and Kawai (2022), §2.2, Eqs. (1), (8) and (9) give the following semidiscrete system: space is discrete while time remains continuous. E is total energy per unit volume.
\begin{aligned}
\boldsymbol q_{i,j}&=(\rho,\rho u,\rho v,E)^{\mathsf T}_{i,j},\\
\frac{d\boldsymbol q_{i,j}}{dt}
&=-\frac{\widehat{\boldsymbol F}^{\,x}_{i+1/2,j}-\widehat{\boldsymbol F}^{\,x}_{i-1/2,j}}{\Delta x}
-\frac{\widehat{\boldsymbol F}^{\,y}_{i,j+1/2}-\widehat{\boldsymbol F}^{\,y}_{i,j-1/2}}{\Delta y},\\
\widehat{\boldsymbol F}^{\,x}_{i+1/2,j}
&=\boldsymbol F^{(x)}(\boldsymbol q_{i,j},\boldsymbol q_{i+1,j}),\\
\widehat{\boldsymbol F}^{\,y}_{i,j+1/2}
&=\boldsymbol F^{(y)}(\boldsymbol q_{i,j},\boldsymbol q_{i,j+1}).
\end{aligned}

Dissipation changes amplitude; dispersion changes phase speed. A spatial operator can avoid artificial damping and still move different wavelengths at incorrect speeds. To isolate that distinction, linearize the symmetric, consistent two-point flux about one uniform state q₀. Symmetry and F(q,q)=f(q) give equal left and right Jacobians. For a scalar characteristic wave with speed a, the result is the centered advection equation:
The von Neumann foundations explain the general procedure for reading dissipation and dispersion from an amplification factor’s magnitude and argument. Here that procedure is applied to KEEP linearized about a uniform state.
\begin{aligned}\partial_1 F(q_0,q_0)&=\partial_2 F(q_0,q_0)=\tfrac12 A,\qquad A=f'(q_0),\\\dot v_i&=-a\frac{v_{i+1}-v_{i-1}}{2h}.\end{aligned}
For an individual Fourier mode, with θ=kh, substitution gives
\begin{aligned}v_i(t)&=\widehat v\,e^{\mathrm i(ki h-\omega_h t)},\qquad\theta=kh,\\\lambda_h&=-\mathrm i\,a\frac{\sin\theta}{h},\qquad\omega_h=a\frac{\sin\theta}{h}.\end{aligned}
The eigenvalue is purely imaginary, so this semidiscrete linear mode has no amplitude decay. Its phase and group velocities are nevertheless
\begin{aligned}\frac{c_{\rm ph}}a&=\frac{\omega_h}{ak}=\frac{\sin\theta}{\theta},\\\frac{c_{\rm g}}a&=\frac1a\frac{d\omega_h}{dk}=\cos\theta,\\\frac{\sin\theta}{\theta}&=1-\frac{\theta^2}{6}+O(\theta^4).\end{aligned}

The displayed solver uses classical four-stage RK4. For a frozen linear mode with eigenvalue −iωh, one step multiplies the amplitude by G. This follows directly from the RK4 stages; it is an analysis of the integrator used here, not of a later fully conservative temporal KEEP scheme.
\begin{aligned}v^{n+1}&=Gv^n,\qquad G=R(-\mathrm i\xi),\qquad\xi=\omega_h\Delta t,\\R(z)&=1+z+\frac{z^2}{2}+\frac{z^3}{6}+\frac{z^4}{24}.\end{aligned}
Its squared amplitude gain is exactly
\begin{gathered}|G|^2=1-\frac{\xi^6}{72}+\frac{\xi^8}{576},\\|G|\le1\ \Longleftrightarrow\ |\xi|\le2\sqrt2\quad(\xi\in\mathbb R).\end{gathered}
Strictly inside this interval, a nonzero ξ gives |G| smaller than one. Thus RK4 damps this otherwise undamped spatial mode. Gain is one at ξ=0 and at the two endpoints; beyond them it exceeds one. This pure-imaginary linear-mode stability limit is not a global CFL or positivity guarantee for the nonlinear, variable-state, two-dimensional solver.
Phase must also be checked. Define arg₀ by continuous continuation from ξ=0 rather than by a wrapped principal angle. The fully discrete frequency Ω and the amplitude after n equal steps satisfy
\begin{aligned}\Omega\Delta t&=-\arg_0R(-\mathrm i\xi),\qquad |v^n|=|G|^n|v^0|,\\\Omega\Delta t&=\xi-\frac{\xi^5}{120}+O(\xi^7).\end{aligned}
